    {
      "id": "01",
      "title": "Iterable vs iterator",
      "question": "What is the difference between an iterable and an iterator?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Python generators, question one. What is the difference between an iterable and an iterator? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "What's the difference between an <em>iterable</em> and an <em>iterator</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "Every for loop runs this protocol. It calls iter on the object once, which asks the iterable for an iterator. Then it calls next, again and again, and each call returns the next item. When there are no items left, next raises stop iteration, and the loop catches it and ends.",
          "props": {
            "title": "What a <em>for</em> loop really does",
            "file": "for_loop.py",
            "tag": "iterator protocol",
            "lang": "python",
            "size": 25,
            "width": 1060,
            "code": "# for x in obj: body\nit = iter(obj)              # obj.__iter__()\nwhile True:\n    try:\n        x = next(it)        # it.__next__()\n    except StopIteration:\n        break\n    body(x)",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "once",
                "lines": "2",
                "note": "iter(): the iterable hands out an iterator"
              },
              {
                "at": "again",
                "lines": "5",
                "note": "next(): one item per call"
              },
              {
                "at": "left",
                "lines": "6-7",
                "note": "Exhausted → StopIteration ends the loop"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "So an iterable makes iterators, and should hand out a fresh one every time, so you can loop over it repeatedly. An iterator makes values. Its own iter returns itself, and once it is exhausted, it stays exhausted.",
          "props": {
            "title": "One makes iterators, <em>one makes values</em>",
            "lang": "python",
            "left": {
              "at": "fresh",
              "label": "Iterable",
              "title": "Reusable",
              "tone": "good",
              "code": "words = [\"the\", \"quick\"]\nlist(words)   # ['the', 'quick']\nlist(words)   # ['the', 'quick']",
              "body": "__iter__ returns a fresh iterator each time."
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "exhausted",
              "label": "Iterator",
              "title": "Single-use",
              "code": "it = iter(words)\nlist(it)      # ['the', 'quick']\nlist(it)      # []  — exhausted",
              "body": "__iter__ returns self; __next__ produces values."
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
