    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Laziness and yield from",
      "question": "Why are generators memory-cheap, and what does yield from do?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. Why are generators so cheap in memory, and what does yield from do? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Why are generators <em>memory-cheap</em>, and what does <em>yield from</em> do?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "The eager version calls find all in the constructor, and builds the whole word list before you ask for a single word. The lazy version keeps only the text, and produces each word on demand. On a one gigabyte log, that is the difference between constant memory and loading everything at once.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Eager list, <em>lazy stream</em>",
            "lang": "python",
            "left": {
              "at": "eager",
              "label": "Eager",
              "title": "Builds every word now",
              "tone": "bad",
              "code": "class Sentence:\n    def __init__(self, text):\n        self.words = WORD_RE.findall(text)\n    def __iter__(self):\n        yield from self.words"
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "lazy",
              "label": "Lazy",
              "title": "One word per next()",
              "tone": "good",
              "code": "class Sentence:\n    def __init__(self, text):\n        self.text = text\n    def __iter__(self):\n        for m in WORD_RE.finditer(self.text):\n            yield m.group()"
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "difference",
              "text": "Lazy: constant memory, even on a 1 GB log.",
              "code": "O(1) memory"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "Laziness also makes infinite sequences safe: just stop pulling, here with eye-slice. And yield from delegates to another iterable, yielding everything it produces. That makes recursive flattening a three-line function.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Infinite streams and <em>yield from</em>",
            "file": "lazy.py",
            "tag": "itertools",
            "lang": "python",
            "size": 23,
            "width": 1080,
            "code": "def progression(begin, step):\n    while True:                 # never ends…\n        yield begin\n        begin += step\n\nlist(itertools.islice(progression(1, 2), 5))  # [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]\n\ndef flatten(items):\n    for item in items:\n        if isinstance(item, (list, tuple)):\n            yield from flatten(item)   # delegate\n        else:\n            yield item",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "infinite",
                "lines": "1-4",
                "note": "An endless generator is fine…"
              },
              {
                "at": "pulling",
                "lines": "6",
                "note": "…take only what you need"
              },
              {
                "at": "delegates",
                "lines": "11",
                "note": "yield from: yield everything it yields"
              },
              {
                "at": "flattening",
                "lines": "8-13",
                "note": "[1, [2, [3]]] → 1, 2, 3"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "recap",
          "say": "To recap. An iterable makes iterators; an iterator makes values. A for loop calls iter once, then next until stop iteration. A generator function returns a generator that freezes at each yield. And laziness gives constant memory, and infinite streams.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Generators, <em>in four lines</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "makes",
                "text": "Iterables make iterators; iterators make values.",
                "code": "__iter__ / __next__"
              },
              {
                "at": "once",
                "text": "for: iter() once, next() until StopIteration.",
                "code": "the protocol"
              },
              {
                "at": "freezes",
                "text": "yield hands out a value and freezes the frame.",
                "code": "resume on next()"
              },
              {
                "at": "laziness",
                "text": "Laziness: constant memory, infinite streams.",
                "code": "islice · yield from"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
